    <h2 id="browsing-packs">Browsing Packs</h2>

    <p>Use the <code>browse_packs</code> tool to see all available packs. You can filter by keyword.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Ask your agent:</span>
<span class="dim">"What skill packs are available?"</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent calls browse_packs():</span>
browse_packs()

<span class="cmt"># Or filter by keyword:</span>
browse_packs({ <span class="kw">query</span>: <span class="str">"security"</span> })</div>

    <p>The response includes pack names, descriptions, skill counts, and install commands. The agent can present this as a list and ask which packs you want to install.</p>

    <h2 id="installing-packs">Installing Packs</h2>

    <p>Install a pack by name. The agent calls <code>install_pack</code> with the pack name.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Ask your agent:</span>
<span class="dim">"Install the security-hardening and api-design packs."</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent calls:</span>
install_pack({ <span class="kw">pack</span>: <span class="str">"security-hardening"</span> })
install_pack({ <span class="kw">pack</span>: <span class="str">"api-design"</span> })</div>

    <p>For each pack, the installer:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Downloads</strong> the pack from the community repository.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Validates</strong> each skill's frontmatter and structure.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Checks content guard</strong> compliance for all skill content.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Deduplicates</strong> against existing skills. Skills with matching names are skipped.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Signs</strong> each new skill with your local Ed25519 key.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Writes</strong> skills to <code>~/.mcp-librarian/skills/{pack-name}/</code>.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Rebuilds</strong> the BM25 index to include the new skills.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="info-box">
      <strong>Deduplication:</strong> If you already have a skill with the same name (from a previous install or manual creation), the installer skips it. This preserves any local modifications. To force a reinstall, delete the existing skill file first.
    </div>

    <h3>Installing from a URL</h3>

    <p>You can also install packs from a direct URL instead of the community repository. This is useful for private team packs or third-party pack sources.</p>

    <div class="code-block">install_pack({ <span class="kw">pack</span>: <span class="str">"https://github.com/yourteam/internal-packs/releases/download/v1.0/team-patterns.tar.gz"</span> })</div>

    <h2 id="creating-packs">Creating Your Own Pack</h2>

    <p>Use <code>export_pack</code> to bundle selected skills into a shareable pack.</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="cmt"># Ask your agent:</span>
<span class="dim">"Export my deployment, code-review, and incident-runbook skills as a pack called team-patterns."</span>

<span class="cmt"># The agent calls:</span>
export_pack({
  <span class="kw">name</span>: <span class="str">"team-patterns"</span>,
  <span class="kw">skills</span>: [<span class="str">"deployment-process"</span>, <span class="str">"code-review-checklist"</span>, <span class="str">"incident-runbook"</span>],
  <span class="kw">description</span>: <span class="str">"Internal team patterns and runbooks"</span>
})</div>

    <p>This creates a directory at <code>~/.mcp-librarian/exports/team-patterns/</code> containing the skill files and a <code>pack.json</code> manifest.</p>

    <h2 id="pack-manifest">Pack Manifest Format</h2>

    <p>The <code>pack.json</code> manifest describes the pack and lists its contents. This file is created automatically by <code>export_pack</code> but can also be written by hand.</p>

    <div class="code-block">{
  <span class="kw">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"team-patterns"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"version"</span>: <span class="str">"1.0.0"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"description"</span>: <span class="str">"Internal team patterns and runbooks"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"author"</span>: <span class="str">"your-team"</span>,
  <span class="kw">"skills"</span>: [
    {
      <span class="kw">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"deployment-process"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"file"</span>: <span class="str">"deployment-process.md"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"description"</span>: <span class="str">"CI/CD pipeline, environment configs, rollback procedures"</span>
    },
    {
      <span class="kw">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"code-review-checklist"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"file"</span>: <span class="str">"code-review-checklist.md"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"description"</span>: <span class="str">"What to check in code reviews, common pitfalls"</span>
    },
    {
      <span class="kw">"name"</span>: <span class="str">"incident-runbook"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"file"</span>: <span class="str">"incident-runbook.md"</span>,
      <span class="kw">"description"</span>: <span class="str">"Incident response steps, escalation, post-mortem template"</span>
    }
  ]
}</div>

    <h3>Manifest Fields</h3>

    <table class="cmd-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>name</td><td>Yes</td><td>Pack identifier. Lowercase with hyphens.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>version</td><td>Yes</td><td>Semantic version of the pack.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>description</td><td>Yes</td><td>Brief description of what the pack contains.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>author</td><td>No</td><td>Author or team name.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>skills</td><td>Yes</td><td>Array of skill entries with name, file, and description.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="contributing">Contributing Packs</h2>

    <p>To contribute a pack to the community repository:</p>

    <ol>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Create your skills.</strong> Write well-structured skills following the <a href="/librarian/wiki/skill-format/">Skill Format</a> guide. Each skill should pass <code>validate_skill</code>.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Export as a pack.</strong> Use <code>export_pack</code> to generate the pack directory and manifest.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Test the pack.</strong> Install the pack from your local export directory to verify it installs cleanly and all skills are indexed correctly.</li>
      <li><strong style="color: var(--text-1);">Open a pull request.</strong> Fork <code>penumbraforge/mcp-librarian-skills</code>, add your pack directory, and open a PR with a description of what the pack covers.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Pack review criteria:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>All skills must pass <code>validate_skill</code> with no issues.</li>
      <li>All skills must pass the <a href="/librarian/wiki/content-guard/">content guard</a> check.</li>
      <li>Skills should be well-structured with descriptive headings, code examples, and focused sections.</li>
      <li>The pack should cover a coherent topic area (not a random assortment of skills).</li>
      <li>Content should be accurate and up to date.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="pack-directory-structure">Pack Directory Structure</h2>

    <p>Installed packs are stored in subdirectories under the skills directory:</p>

    <div class="code-block"><span class="dim">~/.mcp-librarian/</span>
<span class="dim">  skills/</span>
<span class="dim">    security-hardening/</span>
<span class="dim">      sql-injection.md</span>
<span class="dim">      xss-prevention.md</span>
<span class="dim">      input-validation.md</span>
<span class="dim">      ...</span>
<span class="dim">    api-design/</span>
<span class="dim">      error-handling.md</span>
<span class="dim">      pagination.md</span>
<span class="dim">      ...</span>
<span class="dim">    graphql-federation.md</span>       <span class="cmt"># standalone skill (no pack)</span></div>

    <p>Standalone skills (created via <code>create_skill</code> or auto-research) live directly in the <code>skills/</code> directory. Pack skills live in their pack's subdirectory. Both are indexed identically by the BM25 search engine.</p>
